      "content": "You're absolutely right that Manhattan's grid isn't designed solely for vehicles; its perpendicular streets create natural visual and directional channels often aligned with historic landmarks. At the intersection of Broadway and Chambers Street, the sharp right-angle turn of Broadway (a diagonal grid axis) creates a highly disruptive geometric node compared to the city's regular gridded streets. This forces pedestrians and drivers to stop, cross, and change orientation abruptly, which cognitively acts exactly as the shape of attention and navigation a node you're describing. Chelsea Market and its associated rail lines have historically cemented this area as a massive and unavoidable focus of human movement. It seems the architecture deliberately uses these forced stops to funnel attention into specific commercial and social hubs.",
